          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My TSH is a little high but my free T4 is still in the normal range. What does that mean, what does guidance describe about treating it, and how often should it be rechecked?",
          "answer": "A raised TSH with a normal free T4 is called **subclinical hypothyroidism**. The Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance (Hypothyroidism: which test comes first) explains that TSH is the first test because it reacts to small changes in thyroid hormone. When TSH is raised, free T4 is measured. A normal free T4 is the pattern that guidance labels subclinical rather than overt. Antibody tests can show whether an autoimmune thyroid condition is the cause and how likely the change is to progress. This is a description of the category, not a statement about your own results.\n\n**Treatment:** The guidance says experts disagree. There is broad agreement to treat when TSH stays above about 10 mIU/L. For values between the upper reference limit and 10, some guidance suggests a trial of treatment if symptoms, positive antibodies, or cardiovascular risk are present. Other guidance suggests monitoring alone for most people. Pregnancy is handled separately with lower treatment thresholds. The retrieved text does not say which approach applies to you.\n\n**Rechecking:** I could not find a recheck interval for a raised TSH with normal free T4 that is not on treatment. The only interval in the results is the general hypothyroidism note (Hypothyroidism, underactive thyroid), which says dose changes are rechecked six to eight weeks after the change. That applies to people on replacement treatment, so it does not answer your question directly.\n\nBecause the results do not cover your situation, ask your clinician whether treatment is recommended for you, whether antibody tests would help, and when the next TSH test should be.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
